<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!-- V8 compiles JavaScript into MAP_JIT pages, which the hardened runtime
         only permits with this entitlement. Electron has needed nothing beyond
         it since 11.1.0, and @electron/osx-sign grants exactly this key for
         darwin, so the electron-builder default template's two extra keys are
         not requirements of this app.

         This file is for a signature Apple issued, which is the only kind that
         carries a team identifier for library validation to compare against.
         Nothing in this repository can mint one today, so every build takes
         build/entitlements.mac.adhoc.plist instead; the day a Developer ID
         signature exists, that exception is withdrawn and this file applies.

         Everything else is deliberately withheld. The main process holds the
         account session token and writes paid API keys into the CLI config
         files, so library validation - the hardened runtime's main defence
         against loading attacker-supplied dylibs into that process - must stay
         on. This app bundles no third-party native module (every runtime
         dependency is pure JavaScript) and the native helpers it ships are
         spawned as separate processes, so nothing here needs to dlopen foreign
         code. Grant a new key only together with the reason it became
         unavoidable. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
